WEBVTT

00:00:03.629 --> 00:00:10.630
<v Sarah Okafor>Morning, everyone. Wednesday the 23rd. Can you all hear me, or am I talking to the little spinning circle again?</v>

00:00:11.689 --> 00:00:18.339
<v Grace Liu>We've got you. More importantly, whose new car is that in the car park? Very shiny for this place.</v>

00:00:19.429 --> 00:00:24.679
<v Jamie Brennan>Someone's new car, definitely not mine. Mine's the one with the permanently illuminated engine light.</v>

00:00:25.559 --> 00:00:28.010
<v Tomasz Nowak>That's not a fault, that's mood lighting.</v>

00:00:28.390 --> 00:00:32.590
<v Sarah Okafor>Right, before this turns into a car clinic. Finished bits first, please.</v>

00:00:33.100 --> 00:00:38.350
<v Marcus Hale>Yeah, so I already swapped the filter on the Line 2 Safety Guard Interlock yesterday.</v>

00:00:38.799 --> 00:00:42.299
<v Hannah Price>That's finished, then? Not something still hanging over from yesterday?</v>

00:00:43.420 --> 00:00:47.619
<v Marcus Hale>Finished, yeah. Just reporting it, not putting it back on the list.</v>

00:00:48.920 --> 00:00:54.170
<v Tomasz Nowak>And I signed off the inspection on the AC1 on Tuesday. That's closed as well.</v>

00:00:54.590 --> 00:00:58.090
<v Sarah Okafor>Lovely. Two things we don't need to go round again.</v>

00:00:58.710 --> 00:01:04.659
<v Aisha Begum>Um, I noticed the line three was running a bit warm on nights, but it settled down.</v>

00:01:06.340 --> 00:01:10.540
<v Hannah Price>Settled before the end of nights? Sorry, you clipped a bit there.</v>

00:01:12.060 --> 00:01:16.609
<v Aisha Begum>Yeah, it settled down. Just an observation, nothing I'm raising as new work.</v>

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:25.000
<v Marcus Hale>Same sort of thing from me: heard a slight rattle from the line 1 mould temp. Nothing to action yet.</v>

00:01:26.390 --> 00:01:30.590
<v Sarah Okafor>Okay, observations only on those. Not treating either as an extra job.</v>

00:01:32.359 --> 00:01:38.310
<v Tomasz Nowak>The other one, I can't get to the gas turbine two until production gives us a window.</v>

00:01:39.689 --> 00:01:43.540
<v Sarah Okafor>Still blocked, then. We're not allocating that as work for today.</v>

00:01:45.049 --> 00:01:47.849
<v Tomasz Nowak>Exactly. Without the window, it's not going anywhere.</v>

00:01:49.370 --> 00:01:57.420
<v Hannah Price>And we were going to replace the belt on the line 2 robot, but that's been pushed to next month, so ignore it.</v>

00:01:57.959 --> 00:02:01.109
<v Jamie Brennan>Right, so that isn't lurking on today's list either?</v>

00:02:02.019 --> 00:02:04.819
<v Hannah Price>No. Off today's list. Next month, not now.</v>

00:02:06.489 --> 00:02:13.139
<v Sarah Okafor>Actual actions, then. Jamie Brennan, can you check the guarding on the IM3 on the 3rd? It's fairly important.</v>

00:02:13.789 --> 00:02:17.639
<v Jamie Brennan>Yeah, just checking the guarding on the IM3, that's the scope?</v>

00:02:20.060 --> 00:02:22.860
<v Sarah Okafor>That's right, the guarding check. On the 3rd.</v>

00:02:24.949 --> 00:02:29.500
<v Jamie Brennan>Okay, I'll check the guarding on the IM3 on the 3rd. Got it.</v>

00:02:30.370 --> 00:02:35.620
<v Grace Liu>And fairly important, as you said. Don't want that bit getting lost in the audio.</v>

00:02:37.659 --> 00:02:40.810
<v Sarah Okafor>Yes, fairly important. That's the priority for that one.</v>

00:02:41.599 --> 00:02:47.900
<v Marcus Hale>I'll recalibrate the sensors on the Line 3 Chiller today at 2pm. It matters but it's not urgent.</v>

00:02:49.960 --> 00:02:53.810
<v Aisha Begum>Marc, that's an actual recalibration, rather than just comparing the readings?</v>

00:02:55.740 --> 00:02:58.539
<v Marcus Hale>Yeah, recalibrate the sensors. That's what I'm taking.</v>

00:03:00.509 --> 00:03:02.259
<v Sarah Okafor>Okay, today at 2pm, Marc?</v>

00:03:03.050 --> 00:03:04.449
<v Marcus Hale>Today at 2pm, yep.</v>

00:03:05.509 --> 00:03:09.009
<v Hannah Price>Got you. Matters, but not urgent. I heard the distinction.</v>

00:03:09.889 --> 00:03:13.389
<v Sarah Okafor>Right. Gas valve sign-off next, different priority on this one.</v>

00:03:15.710 --> 00:03:22.710
<v Sarah Okafor>I'll do the lock-out tag-out sign-off on the turbine one gas valve on the 3rd. Drop everything for that one.</v>

00:03:24.090 --> 00:03:26.889
<v Grace Liu>Saz, sorry—on the 3rd for that sign-off, yes?</v>

00:03:27.370 --> 00:03:32.620
<v Sarah Okafor>Yes, on the 3rd. Mine, and drop everything for that one. The date hasn't changed.</v>

00:03:33.439 --> 00:03:36.590
<v Marcus Hale>Understood. Date and priority are two separate bits there.</v>

00:03:37.849 --> 00:03:44.500
<v Sarah Okafor>Marc, can you organise the toolbox talk for the team tomorrow? Not an emergency but don't let it slip.</v>

00:03:46.030 --> 00:03:51.979
<v Marcus Hale>Ah, no, sorry. I can't take that one as well. Haven't got the capacity to own it.</v>

00:03:52.500 --> 00:04:00.550
<v Sarah Okafor>Fair enough. Jamie, can you take that instead—organise the toolbox talk for the team tomorrow? Not an emergency but don't let it slip.</v>

00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:06.020
<v Jamie Brennan>Yeah, I'll take it instead. I'll organise the toolbox talk for the team tomorrow.</v>

00:04:07.449 --> 00:04:10.949
<v Hannah Price>Sorry, I spoke over the end there. Tomorrow, not today?</v>

00:04:12.150 --> 00:04:16.350
<v Jamie Brennan>Tomorrow, yeah. And understood: not an emergency, but don't let it slip.</v>

00:04:18.389 --> 00:04:25.740
<v Sarah Okafor>One still needing an owner: take vibration readings on the turbine one compressor today. Normal priority. Anyone able to take that?</v>

00:04:27.110 --> 00:04:31.310
<v Tomasz Nowak>I can't take it, sorry. Don't put my name against that one.</v>

00:04:32.209 --> 00:04:34.660
<v Marcus Hale>Nor me. Same capacity problem, I'm afraid.</v>

00:04:35.769 --> 00:04:37.870
<v Jamie Brennan>Can't take that on top, no.</v>

00:04:38.389 --> 00:04:40.839
<v Aisha Begum>I can't commit to that today either.</v>

00:04:43.329 --> 00:04:45.430
<v Grace Liu>Not one I can take, sorry.</v>

00:04:46.810 --> 00:04:48.910
<v Hannah Price>No, I can't own that one.</v>

00:04:49.480 --> 00:04:58.930
<v Sarah Okafor>And I haven't got capacity for it either. So the vibration readings on the turbine one compressor are still needed today, normal priority, but nobody owns them.</v>

00:04:59.379 --> 00:05:04.629
<v Aisha Begum>Yeah, leave that visibly unowned. Better than it looking agreed to someone when it isn't.</v>

00:05:06.250 --> 00:05:09.050
<v Sarah Okafor>Exactly. Needed, but unallocated. No name against it.</v>

00:05:09.790 --> 00:05:21.339
<v Jamie Brennan>Just so I've not muddled mine: guarding on the IM3 on the 3rd, fairly important. Organise the toolbox talk for the team tomorrow, not an emergency but don't let it slip. Both accepted.</v>

00:05:22.689 --> 00:05:28.990
<v Marcus Hale>And mine's recalibrate the sensors on the Line 3 Chiller today at 2pm. Matters but it's not urgent.</v>

00:05:31.040 --> 00:05:38.740
<v Sarah Okafor>Yes. And I'll do the lock-out tag-out sign-off on the turbine one gas valve on the 3rd. Drop everything for that one.</v>

00:05:39.310 --> 00:05:43.860
<v Tomasz Nowak>Fine. And the gas turbine two stays blocked, not quietly added to mine.</v>

00:05:45.500 --> 00:05:50.399
<v Hannah Price>Same with the line 2 robot belt: pushed to next month. Not today's work.</v>

00:05:51.709 --> 00:05:54.860
<v Grace Liu>Good. Still no confession about the new car, then.</v>

00:05:55.569 --> 00:05:58.720
<v Jamie Brennan>Whoever owns it is probably pretending their microphone's broken.</v>

00:05:59.829 --> 00:06:02.980
<v Sarah Okafor>Probably the sensible approach. Right, thanks all. That's us.</v>
